A||N||1||Bigfieroman||Oil light?||||5||
Z||000000||Bigfieroman||04-29-2001||09:25 AM||||Recently, after driving around my suburban area for about an hour, my oil pressure dropped low.  I was sitting at a light for about 2 minutes, and it began flashing on and off because it was just on the limit of low pressure.  Any throttle application remedied the problem, and it is never even close to that low when I first start it.  I have checked the oil level, and it is right between the full and add marks.  I did add one of those leak stopping additives about 700 miles ago, and I am begining to fear it is blocking all the oil passages.  I specifically looked for one without any solids in it, but you never know.  Can anyone help me out on this? Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierohoho||04-29-2001||08:52 PM||||Bigfieroman, don't worry about blocked oil passages. If they were blocked your oil presure would be fine, (nowhere for it to go).<BR>It's not uncommon for the oil presure to be OK at start up, the oil is thicker when its cold, as it heats up it thins a little causing the oil presure to become low. The problem is engine wear, the bearings and mating sufaces have a little more room, the oil squirts through a little easier. <BR>You could try switching to a heavier weight oil. If you now use 5W30 try a 10W40. <BR>Also, don't discount a bad oil presure sending unit that reads low or a worn oil pump. It could be any number of things. The cheapest check is the heavier weight oil. Good luck.||||reg||1
